{#
  MEDIA SLOT — one figure that is either the real asset or a labelled stand-in.

  WHY IT SWITCHES ON `present` RATHER THAN ON A FLAG SOMEBODY MAINTAINS.
  _data/onton.js resolves `present` by looking for the file on disk, so the only
  thing that turns a placeholder into artwork is the artwork arriving. There is
  no second edit to forget, and no state where the page claims an image exists
  and 404s. Same contract the My Work thumbnails already use.

  THE BOX IS THE SAME SIZE EITHER WAY. Both branches sit inside one figure
  carrying the Figma box as an aspect-ratio, so a placeholder reserves exactly
  the space its asset will occupy and dropping the file in shifts nothing on the
  page. That is the whole point of sizing these from the comp rather than from
  whatever the eventual export happens to be.

  VIDEO IS WIRED FOR work-motion.js AND NOTHING ELSE. data-work-motion is the
  attribute that script already binds site-wide: poster deferred via data-poster
  and fetched 400px before the slot arrives, playback started only at 25%
  visibility, paused off-screen and on a hidden tab, and — the reason there is
  no `autoplay` here — never started at all under prefers-reduced-motion, where
  the poster is the presentation. Adding autoplay would defeat that, since it
  fires before any script can read the media query.

  `loop` IS PER SLOT. A clip that ends where it started should loop; one that
  draws itself in and finishes somewhere else must not, or the finished frame
  snaps back to an empty one. The second kind pairs `loop: false` with
  data-motion-replay so it holds its last frame and runs again on re-entry —
  see the note in work-motion.js for why the replay is opt-in.

  macros:
    mediaSlot(m)  m is an image()/video() entry from _data/onton.js
    markSlot(m)   m is a mark() entry — small inline logos and persona markers
#}

{% macro mediaSlot(m, modifier="") %}
  <figure
    class="case-media{% if modifier %} case-media--{{ modifier }}{% endif %}"
    style="--media-w: {{ m.w }}; --media-h: {{ m.h }}"
  >
    {%- if m.present and m.kind == "image" %}
      <img
        class="case-media__asset"
        src="{{ m.url }}"
        alt="{{ m.alt }}"
        width="{{ m.w | round }}"
        height="{{ m.h | round }}"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        draggable="false"
      />
    {%- elif m.present and m.kind == "video" %}
      <video
        class="case-media__asset"
        data-work-motion
        {% if m.posterPresent %}data-poster="{{ m.poster }}"{% endif %}
        width="{{ m.w | round }}"
        height="{{ m.h | round }}"
        role="img"
        aria-label="{{ m.alt }}"
        {% if m.loop %}loop{% endif %}
        {%- if m.replayOnEnter %} data-motion-replay{% endif %}
        muted
        playsinline
        preload="none"
        disablepictureinpicture
        draggable="false"
      >
        <source src="{{ m.webm }}" type="video/webm" />
        <source src="{{ m.mp4 }}" type="video/mp4" />
      </video>
    {%- else %}
      {#
        THE STAND-IN. Deliberately plain and deliberately loud: it names the
        type and the exact path the asset must land at, so the page itself is
        the drop-in instruction. It is aria-hidden with a role-less wrapper —
        a screen-reader user gains nothing from hearing a filename that does not
        exist yet, and the surrounding prose already carries the meaning.
      #}
      <div class="case-media__slot" aria-hidden="true">
        <p class="case-media__label">{{ m.label }}</p>
        <p class="case-media__path">
          {%- if m.kind == "video" -%}
            {{ m.webm }}<br />{{ m.mp4 }}
          {%- else -%}
            {{ m.url }}
          {%- endif -%}
        </p>
        <p class="case-media__dims">{{ m.w }} × {{ m.h }}</p>
      </div>
    {%- endif %}
  </figure>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  Small marks — the ONTON lockup, the five competitor logos, the persona
  markers. Same present/placeholder switch, but these sit inline in a line of
  text rather than in a ratio box, so the stand-in is a square outline at the
  mark's own size with a title for anyone inspecting it. No initials, no drawn
  glyph, nothing that could be mistaken for the real logo.
#}
{% macro markSlot(m) %}
  {%- if m.present %}
    <img
      class="case-mark"
      src="{{ m.url }}"
      alt=""
      aria-hidden="true"
      width="{{ m.size }}"
      height="{{ m.size }}"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      draggable="false"
      style="--mark-size: {{ m.size }}px"
    />
  {%- else %}
    <span
      class="case-mark case-mark--slot"
      aria-hidden="true"
      title="{{ m.label }} — {{ m.url }}"
      style="--mark-size: {{ m.size }}px"
    ></span>
  {%- endif %}
{% endmacro %}
